{#- How a path is kept out of version control before a skill writes it: the
   check, the `.gitignore` entry, the check again, and the guard that git never
   ignores a tracked path. `pipelex-inputs` says it for the copies of the user's
   files, `pipelex-lab` for a case of the user's own files, and `pipelex-run` for
   the `runs/` folder a save writes into; they used to carry the procedure in
   their own words, and one wording keeps the three guarantees from drifting
   apart: the entry goes in the nearest `.gitignore`, relative to that file's
   directory; it is confirmed with `git check-ignore -q`; and a path still not
   ignored, which a tracked one never is, waits for the user.

   Each skill keeps its own bold subject ("… stays out of version control")
   before the include, and sets what it words differently with `{% set %}`:

     git_ignore_paths — what is checked and when, e.g. "each copy's path before
                        writing it"; it follows `git check-ignore -q` directly
     git_ignore_entry — the `.gitignore` entry written when a path is not ignored

   Neither has a default: strict mode fails the build naming the one left unset.

   Included mid-sentence, after the skill's subject, so it strips its own
   trailing newline (the closing comment below). -#}
in a git repository, `git check-ignore -q` {{ git_ignore_paths }}. For a path not ignored, add {{ git_ignore_entry }} to the nearest `.gitignore`, relative to that file's directory, say so, and check again: **git never ignores a tracked path, so one still not ignored is not written until the user says so.**
{#- -#}
